<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>Read the radar, alarm and Nearby list.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The radar is the page you leave a skyBlip Go on. Here is how to read its symbols, the Nearby list and the alarm.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What you see in flight</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="heading-up">You are in the centre, <span class="highlight">track up</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        You are in the centre, track up. The picture rotates to keep your track at the top. An aircraft shown at the upper right is ahead and to the right of the nose, with no mental conversion needed. The top of the screen is kept clear to show traffic ahead of you. Only <span class="mono">NO TX</span> and the reason transmission has stopped can appear there, in the top-left corner.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each symbol&rsquo;s shape shows the aircraft&rsquo;s altitude relative to you. A point facing up means above you. A point facing down means below. A diamond means at your level. A small angled mark shows if the aircraft is climbing or descending. The line from each symbol ends at its predicted position in a minute.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "radar.png", alt: "The radar page: three aircraft drawn as diamonds, one above, one below and one level and climbing, each with a line showing where it will be in a minute, your own aircraft's projected track dashed with a dot at each of the next two minutes, the four-mile ring, the flight time and the count of aircraft heard" %>
      <figcaption>Radar</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Track up, never north up</li>
    <li>Ring of 1, 2, 4 or 8&nbsp;NM</li>
    <li>The shape says above, level or below</li>
    <li>A minute of track ahead of each aircraft</li>
    <li>The count of every aircraft heard</li>
    <li>Flight time, for the logbook</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="every-aircraft">Every aircraft heard, <span class="highlight">whatever its distance</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        In the to-scale mode, aircraft beyond the edge of the screen are no longer visible. With the 4&nbsp;NM ring, an aircraft beyond about six miles disappears. Setting
        <span class="mono">PLOT</span> to <span class="mono">ALL</span> shows every aircraft heard with a position. It is the first option in the settings menu and the default on a new device. The ring shrinks to leave a band around it. An aircraft beyond the ring appears on that band, in its direction, with the same shape it would have inside.
      </p>
      <p>
        The symbol on the band shows the aircraft's direction, not its distance. Its track line remains to scale. It is calculated from the aircraft's real position, but only the part inside the ring is drawn. An aircraft closing fast from six miles therefore appears on the band with a line already inside the ring. That line ends at its predicted position in a minute.
      </p>
      <p>
        Aircraft close together on the band share one symbol with a count beside it. The symbol of an aircraft triggering an alarm takes priority. Otherwise, the symbol is that of the aircraft closest to your altitude. The count never covers another aircraft. A symbol behind you moves aside so the range label does not hide it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "far-all.png", alt: "The radar page with PLOT ALL: a smaller ring with two aircraft inside it, and on the band around it an aircraft ahead with its projected track entering the ring, a mark with the count 3 for a group to the upper right, and one aircraft far to the left" %>
      <figcaption>PLOT ALL</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>ALL, the default: every aircraft with a position</li>
    <li>TO SCALE: the picture drawn to the ring's scale</li>
    <li>On the band: the direction, the shape, the alarm</li>
    <li>A minute of track, where it enters the ring</li>
    <li>Aircraft grouped on the band share a symbol and a count</li>
    <li>The corner shows the count of every aircraft heard</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    <%= link_to_page "displays", label: "The comparison of both modes", anchor: "both-ways" %>
    shows the same traffic in both modes, one button press apart.
  </p>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="alarm">One alarm that shows <span class="highlight">where to look</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        When an aircraft is within 3&nbsp;km horizontally and 300&nbsp;m vertically, its symbol fills in black. A quarter of the screen centred on its direction goes black once a second. You have nothing to read: the flash catches the corner of your eye and shows which way to look. Whatever page you are on, the Go returns to the radar.
      </p>
      <p>
        The black sector catches the eye. The symbol gives you the precise direction. For an aircraft 38° off the nose, the sector is centred on 38°. You look in that direction, rather than only &ldquo;ahead and right&rdquo; across a quarter of the sky.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no higher alert level, and there never will be. Climbing or
        descending has to be coordinated with the other aircraft. This
        device has no link for that. It shows you where to look without telling you how to manoeuvre.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "alarm.png", alt: "The radar page during a traffic advisory: a black 90-degree sector centred on the direction of the traffic, with the filled diamond of the converging aircraft and its one-minute projected track shown in white against it" %>
      <figcaption>Traffic advisory</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>3&nbsp;km around you, 300&nbsp;m above and below</li>
    <li>A quarter of the screen, centred on the traffic</li>
    <li>One flash a second</li>
    <li>The buzzer, vibration and LED alert you too</li>
    <li>One touch of the pad silences it</li>
    <li>A caution, never an instruction</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="shapes">Six shapes for relative altitude, <span class="highlight">readable without learning them</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    A point facing up means traffic above you. A point facing down means below. A diamond means
    traffic within 60&nbsp;m of your altitude. A full-size symbol means the aircraft is within the 300&nbsp;m
    altitude range used by the alarm. A smaller symbol means it is further away vertically. It
    stays on the picture without ever triggering the alarm. The angled mark appears from
    2.5&nbsp;m/s of climb or descent, pointing in the direction the traffic is going.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The ring means an aircraft is sending no altitude. It points neither up nor down, because
    its altitude relative to you is unknown. Within 3&nbsp;km it is filled
    like any alarm, because it may be at your level.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Black fill is reserved for alarm symbols. While no symbol is filled, the screen is not reporting an urgent situation.
  </p>

  <%= render "shared/blips",
        plain: "Plain",
        alarm: "Alarm",
        climbing: "climbing",
        steady: "steady",
        sinking: "sinking",
        above: "Point up",
        above_when: "above you, to 300 m",
        level: "Diamond",
        level_when: "within 60 m of your level",
        below: "Point down",
        below_when: "below you, to 300 m",
        far_above: "Point up, reduced",
        far_above_when: "above you, beyond 300 m",
        far_below: "Point down, reduced",
        far_below_when: "below you, beyond 300 m",
        unknown: "Ring",
        unknown_when: "no altitude sent",
        out_of_window: "outside the alarm's altitude window" %>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="nearby">The Nearby list, <span class="highlight">those at your height first</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        The radar shows where to look. The Nearby list identifies aircraft with a known position, one per row. Each row shows the aircraft&rsquo;s callsign, or its radio address if it broadcasts no callsign. It also shows the horizontal distance between you and the aircraft&rsquo;s altitude relative to you. Two dashes replace the altitude difference if the aircraft sends no altitude. The radio address appears in small type to distinguish it from a callsign.
      </p>
      <p>
        Above the list, one row shows the Go&rsquo;s status and another shows a paired Bravo&rsquo;s. Each row gives the battery status, the number of satellites used to calculate its position and the number of aircraft received. When a unit has no position or does not work, a message replaces the satellite and traffic counts: <span class="mono">GPS OFF</span>,
        <span class="mono">MOVE OUT</span>, <span class="mono">FINDS FIX</span>,
        <span class="mono">NO LINK</span> or <span class="mono">RX DOWN</span>. A Bravo that relays ADS-B keeps its counts without a position of its own, because its traffic is placed on the Go&rsquo;s position. The radar&rsquo;s top-right corner says the same word as the Bravo row.
      </p>
      <p>
        Aircraft triggering an alarm come first, followed by those within 300&nbsp;m
        (about 1,000&nbsp;ft) of your altitude, then the rest. Within each group, aircraft are sorted by horizontal distance, nearest first. An airliner overhead has a small horizontal distance but appears after a glider at your height.
      </p>
      <p>
        The letter at the start of the row tells you where the information came from.
        <span class="mono">L</span> for an ADS-L message received directly,
        <span class="mono">F</span> for FLARM-compatible traffic,
        <span class="mono">U</span> for an aircraft relayed by a ground station,
        <span class="mono">B</span> for ADS-B received by a paired Bravo.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "nearby.png", alt: "The nearby page: a line for the Go with its battery at 92%, 9 satellites and 5 aircraft received, then five aircraft, those at your height first, each with a letter showing the source of the information, its callsign, or its radio address in small type, its horizontal distance and its altitude relative to you" %>
      <figcaption>Nearby</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="no-tx">When Go stops transmitting, <span class="highlight">it keeps warning you</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        When Go stops transmitting your position, it keeps receiving traffic and warning you. It also keeps feeding your navigation app and recording your flight. After 10&nbsp;s without meeting a transmission requirement, every page shows <span class="mono">NO TX</span> above a reason word, white on black in the top-left corner. <span class="mono">RADIO</span> means the radio is not answering or listening before transmission. <span class="mono">GNSS</span> means the GPS receiver is not calculating positions twice a second, on the second and half-second. <span class="mono">PPS</span> means no pulse from the GPS receiver for the current second. <span class="mono">HELD</span> means every other requirement holds, but Go has held the positions it owed for more than 5&nbsp;s, because none could go out as ADS-L requires. It keeps trying, and sends the next one as soon as it can. If several requirements fail, Go shows the first in that order. A missing position or UTC time never shows the mark, because the radar already shows <span class="mono">NO FIX</span> and the receiver&rsquo;s progress. On the six-dial page the mark keeps to <span class="mono">NO TX</span> alone, and the Bravo corner to <span class="mono">BRAVO</span>, so the speed and altitude below them stay readable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Transmission stops as soon as a requirement fails and resumes on its own when all requirements hold again. With <span class="mono">HELD</span>, Go keeps trying instead, and the status message still goes out. The mark stays until all requirements have been met for 10&nbsp;s. It can therefore remain briefly after transmission resumes. It does not appear over menus or prompts. In flight, Go vibrates once when the mark appears, or on takeoff if it is already shown. Before Go obtains its first GPS position, the radar shows the receiver&rsquo;s progress instead. Radio faults are checked from start-up. The self test page marks the part preventing transmission with <span class="mono">NO TX</span>. The status page&rsquo;s traffic row shows <span class="mono">TX OFF</span> and the reason without waiting 10&nbsp;s. <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %> shows the same reason as the corner mark while connected.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "no-tx.png", alt: "The radar page with NO TX above PPS in the top-left corner, white on black, 15 seconds after the GPS receiver's last pulse. Traffic remains visible." %>
      <figcaption>15&nbsp;s after the last GPS pulse: receiving, not transmitting</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

